## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical NLP task: normalize product names across languages in chat, a common customer-support need.

## Key Concepts

- Multilingual dictionary-based normalization
- Code-switching handling via token-level language tagging
- Lightweight fuzzy matching (Levenshtein) for typos
- In-browser latency constraints and basic privacy controls
- Per-language evaluation plan

## Code Example

```javascript
const dict = {
  en: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" },
  es: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" },
  de: { "iphone": "IPHON-X" }
};

function normalize(token, lang){
  const d = dict[lang] || {};
  if (d[token]) return { canonical_id: d[token], conf: 1.0 };
  // simple fallback: lowercasing + basic similarity could be added here
  return { canonical_id: token, conf: 0.5 };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to add new product names without retraining?
- How to detect language and manage code-switching at runtime?
- How to scale dictionaries across dozens of languages while preserving privacy?

## Why This Is Asked
This question explores the challenge of acronym expansion and text normalization in a multilingual, on-device environment with stringent latency, memory, and privacy requirements.

## Key Concepts
- On-device NLP with privacy-preserving inference
- Lightweight hybrid approach combining rule-based and ML methods
- Multilingual text normalization and tokenization strategies
- End-to-end latency budgeting and performance evaluation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation sketch: dictionary lookup for abbreviations
const abbreviationDict = {
  'eod': 'end of day',
  'fyi': 'for your information',
  'ceo': 'chief executive officer'
};

function normalizeText(text, lang = 'en') {
  // Tokenize and expand abbreviations
  const tokens = text.toLowerCase().split(/\s+/);
  const normalized = tokens.map(token => 
    abbreviationDict[token] || token
  );
  return normalized.join(' ');
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests architecture for privacy-preserving, real-time NLP at the edge in a multilingual enterprise. It emphasizes latency, data governance, explainability, and robust rollout/rollback.

## Key Concepts
- Edge + cloud hybrid architecture with privacy budgets (differential privacy or federated learning)
- Lightweight multilingual encoders (distilled models) for on-device inference and PII redaction
- Streaming governance registry for compliance audit trails
- Drift detection on model outputs and safe rollback via versioned model artifacts

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(text) {
  return text
    .replace(/[a-z0-9._%+-]+@[a-z0-9.-]+\.[a-z]{2,}/gi, '[EMAIL_REDACTED]')
    .replace(/\b\d{3}[-.]?\d{3}[-.]?\d{4}\b/g, '[PHONE_REDACTED]')
    .replace(/\b\d{3}-\d{2}-\d{4}\b/g, '[SSN_REDACTED]');
}

async function processMessage(message, edgeModel) {
  const redacted = redactPII(message.content);
  const prediction = await edgeModel.classify(redacted);

  if (prediction.confidence > 0.9) {
    await registry.log({ 
      violation: prediction.label, 
      redactedContent: redacted,
      timestamp: Date.now() 
    });
  }
  return { flagged: prediction.confidence > 0.9, prediction };
}
```

## Stack Recommendation
- **Edge**: TensorFlow Lite / ONNX Runtime Mobile, distilled XLM-R or mBERT
- **Cloud**: Python backend (FastAPI), Docker/Kubernetes, Redis for streaming
- **Monitoring**: Prometheus + Grafana, MLflow for model versioning
- **Metrics**: P99 latency, precision/recall per language, drift score (KL divergence), privacy budget consumption
